  "text": "To solve for the product of all possible values of \\( k \\), we start by analyzing the given conditions:\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{b + 1} = \\frac{b}{c + 1} = \\frac{c}{a + 1} = k.\n\\]\nThis implies:\n\\[\na = k(b + 1), \\quad b = k(c + 1), \\quad c = k(a + 1).\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\( k \\) such that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) are distinct. Let's substitute \\( a = k(b + 1) \\) into \\( c = k(a + 1) \\):\n\\[\nc = k(k(b + 1) + 1) = k^2(b + 1) + k.\n\\]\nSimilarly, substituting \\( b = k(c + 1) \\) into \\( c = k(a + 1) \\):\n\\[\nc = k(k(a + 1) + 1) = k^2(a + 1) + k.\n\\]\nNow we have two expressions for \\( c \\):\n\\[\nc = k^2(b + 1) + k,\n\\]\n\\[\nc = k^2(a + 1) + k.\n\\]\nEquating the two expressions for \\( c \\):\n\\[\nk^2(b + 1) + k = k^2(a + 1) + k.\n\\]\nSubtracting \\( k \\) from both sides:\n\\[\nk^2(b + 1) = k^2(a + 1).\n\\]\nSince \\( k \\neq 0 \\) (because \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) are distinct), we can divide both sides by \\( k^2 \\):\n\\[\nb + 1 = a + 1.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nb = a.\n\\]\nHowever, this contradicts the condition that \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) are distinct. Therefore, \\( k \\) must be such that the system is consistent. Let's consider the case where \\( k = -1 \\):\n\\[\na = -1(b + 1), \\quad b = -1(c + 1), \\quad c = -1(a + 1).\n\\]\nThis implies:\n\\[\na = -b - 1, \\quad b = -c - 1, \\quad c = -a - 1.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( c = -a - 1 \\) into \\( b = -c - 1 \\):\n\\[\nb = -(-a - 1) - 1 = a + 1 - 1 = a.\n\\]\nAgain, this contradicts the distinctness condition. Therefore, the only valid solution is \\( k = -1 \\).\n\nThe product of all possible values of \\( k \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-1}.\n\\]",
